<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to declarative validation rules

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.Constraints-->
<docs:Constraints xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:def="org.blockml.bml.definition">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to declarative validation rules
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: declarative validation rules, the constraints container, constraint branches, and when
    a constraint is evaluated, grounded in org.blockml.bml.documentation.ConstraintsModel,
    org.blockml.bml.core.Constraint, and org.blockml.bml.definition.ConstrainableDefinition. Body
    content is authored in a03 from those sources.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:ConstraintsModel" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — branch-to-context binding, validation severity, and standard constraints.
    </see>
    <see type="core:Constraint" relation="Requires">
      The semantic validation rule branch this page introduces.
    </see>
    <see type="def:ConstrainableDefinition" relation="Requires">
      The shared constraints container on Blocks and members.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Constraints</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Concept</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <!--1. What a constraint is-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Constraints as declarative rules">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A constraint is a declarative rule that states a condition a Block or
member must satisfy, so BlockML can check that condition consistently instead of
relying on ad hoc validation code. BlockML always validates a constraint instance
itself — its type, properties, and generic binding — regardless of whether the
rule it describes is ever enforced.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Where a constraint is attached determines what it checks: a constraint
carries no separate target, because the surrounding definition — a Block, a
property, an aggregation, an association, or a capability — supplies that
context automatically.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--2. The shared container-->
        <dpage:Section heading="One shared container on Blocks and members">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Every Block definition and every member definition capable of carrying a
constraint exposes the same single constraints container, so a constraint is
declared the same way no matter what kind of definition it is attached to. The
container itself carries no information about what kind of definition it belongs
to; the position where it is embedded determines the constraint's target and
generic binding.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--3. Branches-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Matching a constraint to its member kind">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Constraints are organized into branches, and each branch is compatible
with only certain kinds of definitions: a value constraint checks the value
assigned to a property, while structural constraints check the shape of a
Block definition or of another member such as an aggregation, association, or
capability.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:List>
              <items>
                <dpage:ListItem text="A value constraint checks the value assigned to a single property." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="A block constraint checks the structure of a Block definition itself." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="A member constraint checks the structure of an aggregation, association, or capability defined on a Block." />
              </items>
            </dpage:List>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Placing a constraint on the wrong branch for its embedding context is a
checked error, not just a naming convention that BlockML leaves to the author's
discipline.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--4. Enforcement-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Whether a constraint is enforced">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A constraint always describes its condition, but only an explicit
enforcement setting tells BlockML to reject a model that violates it; without
that setting the constraint stays descriptive, remaining part of the model as
metadata for renderers, editors, and other tools.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>This optional enforcement is different from a suggestion, which never
participates in validation at all regardless of any setting.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--5. Recap-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Attaching a constraint">
          <content>
            <dpage:List>
              <items>
                <dpage:ListItem text="Identify the Block or member definition the rule should apply to." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="Choose the constraint branch compatible with that kind of definition." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="Add an enforcement setting only if the rule should fail validation, and leave it out when the constraint should stay descriptive." />
              </items>
            </dpage:List>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--6. Continuation-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with non-mandatory guidance">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Suggestions" summary="Learn how non-mandatory guidance for editors and renderers differs from a constraint.">
                  <target>docs:Suggestions</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:Constraints>
